  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nNear to slightly below normal temperatures exist today under weak\nflow aloft. While dry conditions prevail for most, isolated to \nscattered light rain showers are forecast this afternoon and \nevening over far south- eastern Montana and northern Wyoming where\nthe influence of a large low pressure system to the south exists.\nLate this evening into tonight, a dry cold front is then expected\nto push any remaining precipitation out of the region and bring \nbreezy conditions to the area. With this, a surface inversion \nshould keep the strongest winds aloft tonight, though higher hills\nand mountains could see gusty winds at times. As the inversion \nbreaks Thursday morning, the stronger winds aloft will then mix \ndown. While wind gusts in the teens to 20s mph will be common for \nmost, wind gusts in the 30s and 40s mph are forecast over far \neastern Montana and northern Wyoming (Rosebud County, MT to \nSheridan County, WY and east). A wind gust of 50+ mph is possible \nover the far eastern portions of Fallon and Carter Counties as \nwell (60 percent chance).\n\nFriday into Saturday, a ridge of high pressure is expected to \nbuild over the region, bringing warm and dry conditions. During \nthis time, windy conditions will exist at times, especially over \nthe mountains and foothills and near/north of the Bighorn Basin \nand Bighorn Canyon. On Sunday, another dry cold front will end the\nwindy conditions across much of the area and bring slightly \ncooler temperatures as compared to Saturday. \n\nA low amplitude ridge looks to build in Monday, allowing dry \nconditions to continue. Ensemble forecasts suggest a break down of\nthe ridge and large scale troughing developing over the western \nUnited States by the middle of the week. This could bring a return\nto cooler conditions with an increased chance of precipitation by\nTuesday or Wednesday. LA",
